{visual: add title id:main_title content:"Principal Component Analysis (PCA) concepts in less than 5 minutes!" position:"center" duration:persist}
Hello, today we're going to be talking about the main ideas behind principal component analysis, and we're going to cover those concepts in less than 5 minutes.
{visual: clear}

{visual: add diagram id:cells content:"A cluster of 15 green circular cells, tightly packed" position:"center" duration:persist}
Let's say we had some normal cells... 
{visual: add text id:psst_cells content:"(psst - if you're not a biologist, these could be people, or cars, or cities, etc...)" position:"bottom center" duration:temporary}
(psst - if you're not a biologist, imagine that these could be people, or cars, or cities, etc... they could be anything)
Even though they look the same, we suspect that there are differences...

{visual: update diagram id:cells target:"6 cells on the left" color:"cyan" description:"Color 6 of the left-most cells light blue/cyan"}
{visual: add highlight id:hl_type1 content:"Highlight cyan cells" position:"around cyan cells" duration:temporary}
{visual: add text id:type1 content:"These might be one type of cell..." position:"left of cyan cells" duration:temporary}
These might be one type of cell... (or one type of person, or car, or city, etc...)

{visual: update diagram id:cells target:"4 cells on the top right" color:"red" description:"Color 4 top-right cells red"}
{visual: add highlight id:hl_type2 content:"Highlight red cells" position:"around red cells" duration:temporary}
{visual: add text id:type2 content:"These might be another type of cell..." position:"right of red cells" duration:temporary}
These might be another type of cell...

{visual: update diagram id:cells target:"5 cells on the bottom right" color:"yellow" description:"Color 5 bottom-right cells yellow"}
{visual: add highlight id:hl_type3 content:"Highlight yellow cells" position:"around yellow cells" duration:temporary}
{visual: add text id:type3 content:"These might be a third type of cell..." position:"right of yellow cells" duration:temporary}
And lastly, these might be a third type of cell.

{visual: update diagram id:cells target:"all cells" color:"green" description:"Change all cells back to green"}
Unfortunately, we can't observe differences from the outside...

{visual: add text id:seq_rna content:"...so we sequence the mRNA in each cell to identify which genes are active. This tells us what the cell is doing." position:"bottom center" duration:persist}
So we sequence the messenger RNA in each cell to identify which genes are active. This tells us what the cell is doing.
{visual: update text id:seq_rna content:"(If they were people, we could measure their height, blood pressure, reading level etc...)\n...so we sequence the mRNA in each cell to identify which genes are active. This tells us what the cell is doing."}
(psst - If they were people, we could measure their weight, blood pressure, reading level etc...)

{visual: clear}
{visual: add text id:data_text content:"Here's the data..." position:"left center" duration:persist}
Okay, here's the data.
{visual: add diagram id:table1 content:"Table data: Columns: Cell1, Cell2, Cell3, Cell4. Rows: Gene1 (3, 0.25, 2.8, 0.1), Gene2 (2.9, 0.8, 2.2, 1.8), Gene3 (2.2, 1, 1.5, 3.2), Gene4 (2, 1.4, 2, 0.3), Gene5 (1.3, 1.6, 1.6, 0), Gene6 (1.5, 2, 2.1, 3), Gene7 (1, 2.2, 1.2, 2.8), Gene8 (1, 2.7, 0.9, 0.3), Gene9 (0.4, 3, 0.6, 0.1)" position:"right center" duration:persist}
Each column shows how much each gene is transcribed in each cell.
{visual: add highlight id:col_highlight content:"Red box around a column" position:"around Cell1 column" duration:persist}
{visual: move highlight id:col_highlight position:"around Cell2 column" duration:persist}
{visual: move highlight id:col_highlight position:"around Cell3 column" duration:persist}
{visual: move highlight id:col_highlight position:"around Cell4 column" duration:persist}
{visual: clear}

{visual: add text id:two_cells content:"For now, let's imagine there were only 2 cells." position:"top center" duration:persist}
{visual: add diagram id:table_2cells content:"Table data: Columns: Cell1, Cell2. Rows: Gene1 (3, 0.25), Gene2 (2.9, 0.8), Gene3 (2.2, 1), Gene4 (2, 1.4), Gene5 (1.3, 1.6), Gene6 (1.5, 2), Gene7 (1, 2.2), Gene8 (1, 2.7), Gene9 (0.4, 3)" position:"right center" duration:persist}
For now, let's imagine there are only 2 cells.
{visual: add diagram id:plot1 content:"A 2D scatter plot with Cell2 on the x-axis and Cell1 on the y-axis, showing blue data points for each gene forming a downward slope" position:"left center" duration:persist}
If we just have two cells, then we can plot the measurements for each gene.

{visual: add highlight id:hl_gene1_table content:"Red box around Gene1 row" position:"Gene1 row in table" duration:persist}
{visual: add highlight id:hl_point1 content:"Highlight around Gene1 point" position:"around top-left most point in the scatter plot" duration:persist}
{visual: add text id:txt_gene1 content:"This gene (Gene1) is highly transcribed in Cell1 and lowly transcribed in Cell2..." position:"above Gene1 point" duration:persist}
This gene, Gene 1, is highly transcribed in cell 1, and lowly transcribed in cell 2.
{visual: remove highlight id:hl_gene1_table}
{visual: remove highlight id:hl_point1}
{visual: remove text id:txt_gene1}

{visual: add highlight id:hl_gene9_table content:"Red box around Gene9 row" position:"Gene9 row in table" duration:persist}
{visual: add highlight id:hl_point9 content:"Highlight around Gene9 point" position:"around bottom-right most point in the scatter plot" duration:persist}
{visual: add text id:txt_gene9 content:"...and this gene (Gene9) is lowly transcribed in Cell1 and highly transcribed in Cell2." position:"below Gene9 point" duration:persist}
And this gene, Gene 9, is lowly transcribed in cell 1, and highly transcribed in cell 2.
{visual: remove highlight id:hl_gene9_table}
{visual: remove highlight id:hl_point9}
{visual: remove text id:txt_gene9}

{visual: add diagram id:line1 content:"A straight black trend line going downwards through the points" position:"over scatter plot" duration:persist}
{visual: update text id:two_cells content:"In general, Cell1 and Cell2 have an inverse correlation. This means that they are probably two different types of cells since they are using different genes."}
In general, cell 1 and cell 2 have an inverse correlation. This means that they are probably two different types of cells, since they are using different genes.

{visual: clear}
{visual: add text id:three_cells content:"Now let's imagine there are 3 cells." position:"top center" duration:persist}
{visual: add diagram id:table_3cells content:"Table data: Columns: Cell1, Cell2, Cell3. Rows: Gene1 (3, 0.25, 2.8), Gene2 (2.9, 0.8, 2.2), Gene3 (2.2, 1, 1.5), Gene4 (2, 1.4, 2), Gene5 (1.3, 1.6, 1.6), Gene6 (1.5, 2, 2.1), Gene7 (1, 2.2, 1.2), Gene8 (1, 2.7, 0.9), Gene9 (0.4, 3, 0.6)" position:"right center" duration:persist}
Now let's imagine there are 3 cells.
{visual: add diagram id:plot12 content:"Scatter plot of Cell1 vs Cell2 with downward trend line" position:"top left" duration:persist}
We've already seen how we can plot the first two cells to see how closely they are related.
{visual: add diagram id:plot13 content:"Scatter plot of Cell1 vs Cell3 with points sloping upwards" position:"middle left" duration:persist}
Now we can also compare cell 1 to cell 3.
{visual: add diagram id:line13 content:"A straight black trend line going upwards through the points" position:"over plot13" duration:persist}
{visual: add text id:txt_plot13 content:"Cell1 and Cell3 are positively correlated, suggesting they are doing similar things." position:"right of plot13" duration:persist}
Cell 1 and cell 3 are positively correlated, suggesting they are doing similar things.
{visual: add diagram id:plot23 content:"Scatter plot of Cell2 vs Cell3 with points sloping downwards" position:"bottom left" duration:persist}
Lastly, we can also compare cell 2 to cell 3.
{visual: add diagram id:line23 content:"A straight black trend line going downwards through the points" position:"over plot23" duration:persist}
{visual: add text id:txt_plot23 content:"The negative correlation suggests that Cell2 is doing something different from Cell3..." position:"right of plot23" duration:persist}
The negative correlation suggests that cell 2 is doing something different from cell 3.

{visual: clear}
{visual: add text id:txt_3d content:"Alternatively, we could try to plot all three cells at once on a 3-dimensional graph." position:"top center" duration:persist}
{visual: add diagram id:plot_3d content:"A 3D graph with 3 axes and blue spherical points" position:"left center" duration:persist}
{visual: add diagram id:table_3cells_2 content:"Table data: Columns: Cell1, Cell2, Cell3. Rows: Gene1 (3, 0.25, 2.8), Gene2 (2.9, 0.8, 2.2), Gene3 (2.2, 1, 1.5), Gene4 (2, 1.4, 2), Gene5 (1.3, 1.6, 1.6), Gene6 (1.5, 2, 2.1), Gene7 (1, 2.2, 1.2), Gene8 (1, 2.7, 0.9), Gene9 (0.4, 3, 0.6)" position:"right center" duration:persist}
Alternatively, we could try to plot all three cells at once on a 3-dimensional graph.
{visual: add highlight id:hl_axis1 content:"Highlight vertical axis" position:"vertical axis of 3d plot" duration:persist}
{visual: add text id:txt_c1 content:"Cell1 could be the vertical axis..." position:"left of vertical axis" duration:persist}
Cell 1 could be the vertical axis...
{visual: add highlight id:hl_axis2 content:"Highlight horizontal axis" position:"horizontal axis of 3d plot" duration:persist}
{visual: add text id:txt_c2 content:"...Cell2 could be the horizontal axis..." position:"below horizontal axis" duration:persist}
Cell 2 could be the horizontal axis...
{visual: add highlight id:hl_axis3 content:"Highlight depth axis" position:"depth axis of 3d plot" duration:persist}
{visual: add text id:txt_c3 content:"...and Cell3 could be 'depth'." position:"above depth axis" duration:persist}
and cell 3 could be depth.
{visual: remove text id:txt_3d}
{visual: add text id:txt_rotate content:"We could then rotate this graph around to see how the cells are related to each other." position:"top left" duration:persist}
We could then rotate this graph around to see how the cells are related to each other.

{visual: clear}
{visual: add diagram id:table_4cells content:"Table data: Columns: Cell1, Cell2, Cell3, Cell4. Rows: Gene1 (3, 0.25, 2.8, 0.1), Gene2 (2.9, 0.8, 2.2, 1.8), Gene3 (2.2, 1, 1.5, 3.2), Gene4 (2, 1.4, 2, 0.3), Gene5 (1.3, 1.6, 1.6, 0), Gene6 (1.5, 2, 2.1, 3), Gene7 (1, 2.2, 1.2, 2.8), Gene8 (1, 2.7, 0.9, 0.3), Gene9 (0.4, 3, 0.6, 0.1)" position:"right center" duration:persist}
{visual: add text id:txt_4cells content:"But what do we do when we have 4 or more cells?" position:"top center" duration:persist}
But what do we do when we have 4 or more cells?
{visual: add diagram id:many_plots content:"A 4x4 grid of many 2-cell scatter plots" position:"left center" duration:persist}
{visual: update text id:txt_4cells content:"Draw tons and tons of 2 cell plots and try to make sense of them all?"}
Draw tons and tons of 2 cell plots and try to make sense of 'em all?
{visual: remove diagram id:many_plots}
{visual: add diagram id:crazy_plot content:"A chaotic graph with multiple axes (Cell1 to Cell5) spreading out with blue spherical points" position:"left center" duration:persist}
{visual: update text id:txt_4cells content:"Or draw some crazy graph that has an axis for each cell and makes our brain explode?"}
Or draw some crazy graph that has an axis for each cell and makes our brain explode?
{visual: remove diagram id:crazy_plot}
{visual: update text id:txt_4cells content:"No, both of those options are just plain silly."}
No, both of those options are just plain silly.

{visual: clear}
{visual: add text id:txt_pca content:"Instead, we draw a Principal Component Analysis (PCA) plot..." position:"top left" duration:persist}
{visual: add diagram id:plot_pca content:"A 2D scatter plot with PC1 on x-axis and PC2 on y-axis, showing blue points forming three clusters" position:"left center" duration:persist}
Instead, we draw a Principal Component Analysis (PCA) plot.
{visual: update text id:txt_pca content:"A PCA plot converts the correlations (or lack there of) among all of the cells into a 2-D graph."}
A PCA plot converts the correlations (or lack there of) among all of the cells into a 2-D graph.
{visual: update text id:txt_pca content:"Cells that are highly correlated cluster together..."}
Cells that are highly correlated cluster together...
{visual: add highlight id:cluster1 content:"Red circle around top left cluster" position:"around top left cluster in plot_pca" duration:persist}
{visual: add text id:txt_cluster1 content:"This cluster of cells are highly correlated with each other..." position:"top left of plot_pca" duration:persist}
This cluster of cells are highly correlated with each other...
{visual: add highlight id:cluster2 content:"Red circle around middle right cluster" position:"around middle right cluster in plot_pca" duration:persist}
{visual: update text id:txt_cluster1 content:"...so are these..." position:"above cluster2"}
So are these...
{visual: add highlight id:cluster3 content:"Red circle around bottom cluster" position:"around bottom cluster in plot_pca" duration:persist}
{visual: update text id:txt_cluster1 content:"...and so are these." position:"above cluster3"}
And so are these.

{visual: remove highlight id:cluster1}
{visual: remove highlight id:cluster2}
{visual: remove highlight id:cluster3}
{visual: remove text id:txt_cluster1}
{visual: update text id:txt_pca content:"To make the clusters easier to see, we can color code them..."}
{visual: update diagram id:plot_pca color:"top left cluster cyan, middle right cluster yellow, bottom cluster red" description:"Color code points in clusters"}
To make the clusters easier to see, we can color code them...

{visual: add diagram id:cells_final content:"A cluster of 15 green circular cells, tightly packed" position:"right center" duration:persist}
{visual: update text id:txt_pca content:"Once we've identified the clusters in the PCA plot, we can go back to the original cells..."}
Once we've identified the clusters in the PCA plot, we can go back to the original cells...
{visual: update diagram id:cells_final color:"cyan for 6 left cells, red for 4 top-right cells, yellow for 5 bottom-right cells" description:"Color the original cells to match PCA clusters"}
{visual: update text id:txt_pca content:"...and see that they represent 3 different types of cells doing 3 different things with their genes!!!"}
and see that they represent 3 different types of cells doing 3 different types of things with their genes!!!
{visual: clear}

{visual: add text id:txt_interpret content:"Here's one last main idea about how to interpret PCA plots:" position:"top center" duration:persist}
{visual: add text id:txt_ranked content:"The axes are ranked in order of importance." position:"below txt_interpret" duration:persist}
{visual: add diagram id:plot_pca2 content:"A PCA plot with three distinct color-coded clusters: cyan top-left, yellow middle-right, red bottom-left" position:"left center" duration:persist}
Here's one last main idea about how to interpret PCA plots: The axes are ranked in order of importance.

{visual: add highlight id:hl_pc1 content:"Highlight PC1 axis" position:"PC1 axis on bottom of plot" duration:persist}
{visual: add text id:txt_diff_pc1 content:"Differences along the first principal component axis (PC1)..." position:"below PC1 axis" duration:persist}
Differences among the first principal component axis (PC1)...
{visual: update text id:txt_diff_pc1 content:"...are more important than differences along the second principal component axis (PC2)."}
are more important than differences along the second principal component axis (PC2).

{visual: remove highlight id:hl_pc1}
{visual: remove text id:txt_diff_pc1}
{visual: update text id:txt_ranked content:"If the plot looked like this..."}
{visual: update diagram id:plot_pca2 content:"The cyan cluster is moved to top-left, red is directly below it, and yellow is far right" description:"Modify plot so cyan and red are separated vertically, cyan and yellow are separated horizontally by same distance"}
If the plot looked like this...

{visual: add highlight id:hl_dist_y content:"Highlight distance between cyan and red clusters" position:"between cyan and red clusters" duration:persist}
{visual: add text id:txt_dist content:"Where the distance between these two clusters..." position:"right of plot_pca2" duration:persist}
where the distance between these two clusters...
{visual: add highlight id:hl_dist_x content:"Highlight distance between red and yellow clusters" position:"between red and yellow clusters" duration:persist}
{visual: update text id:txt_dist content:"...is about the same as the distance between these two clusters..."}
is about the same as the distance between these two clusters...

{visual: remove highlight id:hl_dist_y}
{visual: add highlight id:hl_red_yellow content:"Red box around red and yellow clusters" position:"bottom part of plot" duration:persist}
{visual: update text id:txt_dist content:"...then these two clusters are more different from each other..."}
then these two clusters are more different from each other...

{visual: remove highlight id:hl_red_yellow}
{visual: remove highlight id:hl_dist_x}
{visual: add highlight id:hl_cyan_red content:"Red box around cyan and red clusters" position:"left part of plot" duration:persist}
{visual: update text id:txt_dist content:"...than these two clusters."}
than these two clusters.

{visual: clear}
{visual: add text id:txt_end content:"Before we go, you should know that PCA is just one way to make sense of this type of data. There are lots of other methods that are variations on this theme of 'dimension reduction'." position:"top center" duration:persist}
Before we go, you should know that PCA is just one way to make sense of this type of data. There are lots of other methods that are variations on this theme of "dimension reduction".
{visual: add diagram id:heatmap content:"A large colorful heatmap" position:"bottom left" duration:persist}
{visual: add text id:txt_heatmap content:"Heatmaps" position:"above heatmap" duration:persist}
These methods include heatmaps
{visual: add diagram id:tsne content:"A t-SNE scatter plot" position:"bottom center" duration:persist}
{visual: add text id:txt_tsne content:"t-SNE Plots" position:"above tsne" duration:persist}
t-SNE plots
{visual: add diagram id:mds content:"An MDS scatter plot" position:"bottom right" duration:persist}
{visual: add text id:txt_mds content:"Multi-Dimensional Scaling (MDS)" position:"above mds" duration:persist}
and multi-dimensional scaling (MDS) plots.
Together, these methods are used to reduce complex data into visual summaries that are easier to interpret.
{visual: add text id:txt_note content:"Key idea: PCA is a dimension reduction method that summarizes many measured variables with a smaller number of ranked principal components." position:"bottom full width" duration:persist}
Key idea: PCA is a dimension reduction method that summarizes many measured variables with a smaller number of ranked principal components.

{visual: clear}
{visual: add text id:txt_the_end content:"The End!!!" position:"center" style:"huge, bold" duration:persist}
This concludes the overview of the main ideas behind PCA.
